<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bleda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 445</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bleda was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> who ruled jointly with his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> from the death of their uncle Rua around 435. Together they forced the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to pay large tributes in gold under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-margus">Treaty of Margus</a> and led raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the early 440s. He died around 445, probably killed on Attila's orders, leaving Attila sole ruler.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bleda was a Hunnic ruler and the elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>. As nephews of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rugila">Rugila</a>, Bleda and Attila succeeded him to the throne, and Bleda reigned until his death. Little is known about him. One of the few details concerns the dwarf Zerco, a Moor, whom Bleda acquired after the great Hun campaign of 441. Bleda was greatly amused by Zerco and had a suit of armour made for him so that the dwarf could accompany him on campaign.</p><p>The name is recorded in Greek sources as Bledas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blida">Blidas</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Bleda. Otto Maenchen-Helfen considered it Germanic or Germanized, a short form of names such as Bladardus. Denis Sinor argued that a name starting with a consonant cluster cannot be Altaic, while Omeljan Pritsak derived it from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> word, bilgä, meaning wise or sovereign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>It is not known exactly how Bleda died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> speculated that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> murdered him on a hunting trip. Medieval Hungarian chronicles tell of his death differently. In the Chronicon Pictum of Mark of Kalt, Attila went to Sicambria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and killed his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, throwing the corpse into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, because Buda had named the city Buda's Castle after himself. Attila forbade the name and called it Attila's Capital, the Germans called it Eccylburg, and the Hungarians ignored the ban and called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obuda">Óbuda</a>.</p><p>Hungarian literature knows Bleda as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. According to medieval sources, the name of Buda, the historic capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, comes from him, and so does that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. He has been portrayed by Ettore Manni in a 1954 film with Anthony Quinn, by Leo Gordon in Sign of the Pagan of the same year, and by Tommy Flanagan in a 2001 mini-series.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bleda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Elites practised polygamy, commoners probably monogamy; Priscus saw women moving freely among men, and <b>Bleda</b>'s widow commanded a village through which Roman envoys rode.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Raniero Raineri di <b>Bleda</b>, a monk of Cluny, was elected pope as Paschal II after the death of Pope Urban II the previous month.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-13">13 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
